WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.592 --> 00:00:02.501
♪ Different faces ♪

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.742
♪ Many races ♪

00:00:04.742 --> 00:00:08.950
♪ Living in the sun ♪

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.120
According to advertisers

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.810
like coffee maker Gregg's,
1970s New Zealand was

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:17.430
a harmonious utopia.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.330
And so, 150 years on,

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:21.770
the great, great granddaughter
of Queen Victoria

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.500
arrived in Waitangi

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.210
escorted by a thousand warriors

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.693
in these magnificent long war canoes.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.440
And while the country has long been lauded

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.810
for its integration and tolerance,

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.530
not everyone shared such a rosy view.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.420
In the background, the shouts and yells

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.290
of Maori activist protesters

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.340
as they tried to get closer to the Queen.

00:00:42.544 --> 00:00:45.370
Colonized by the British
in the 19th century,

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.780
the indigenous population, the Maori,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.430
would lose 97% of their land,

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.081
often through deception and violence.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:59.410
Maori have been disadvantaged ever since.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.960
We have horrific health statistics,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.201
education statistics.

00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:09.200
We are 17% of the population,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.168
but make up 52% of the prison population.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.670
Something had to be done to mitigate

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.430
the horrific atrocities
that have been committed.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.280
A reparations program was created

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.090
to reconcile the wrongs of the past

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.040
and formalize settlement agreements

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.728
between the State and the Maori people.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.216
Settlement procedures have contributed

00:01:38.216 --> 00:01:43.150
to empowering Maori in a big way.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:47.010
We're relatively now a wealthy tribe

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.590
with net settlement assets

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.850
of close to 1.6 billion and rising.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.132
They've been able to give
them an economic opportunity

00:01:57.132 --> 00:01:58.810
to start developing something

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.770
that we would've never
been able to before.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.750
So are New Zealand settlement agreements

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.930
an example of how best to
tackle historical grievances?

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.930
There are no easy answers in the search

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.180
for that elusive concept known as justice,

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.287
so long after the event.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.360
The islands in the South West Pacific,

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.150
now known as New Zealand,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.465
were once inhabited only by the Maori.

00:02:27.465 --> 00:02:31.490
They formed tribes and
subtribes, known as iwi and hapu.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.930
As the tentacles of
Western imperialism spread

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.000
around the world in the 18th century,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.350
White settlers landed in what
was then called Aotearoa,

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.180
looking for resources to exploit.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.159
By the start of the 19th century,

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:46.705
it was like the Wild West.

00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:49.030
A lot on them were sealers and whalers.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.295
It was just debauchery all over the place,

00:02:52.295 --> 00:02:55.087
drunkenness, murder,

00:02:55.087 --> 00:03:00.087
every non-civilized behavior was there.

00:03:00.087 --> 00:03:02.680
Our ancestors became
increasingly frustrated

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.680
that they could not get
these people to abide

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.820
by the laws of the country.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.470
In 1840, the British made an agreement

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.550
with over 500 Maori chiefs

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.610
that would forever define
their relationship:

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.720
The Treaty of Waitangi.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.430
The treaty that was entered into in 1840

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.980
was to give over responsibility
from our leaders here

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.760
to the British leader, who at
that time was Queen Victoria.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.730
In addition, Maori chiefs thought

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.590
the treaty would give them more
say on trade and land sales,

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.730
but the language of the
treaty is contentious.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.510
Two versions; one Maori,
one English, didn't help.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.500
The Maori copy promised
they would continue

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.570
to own their lands, forests and fisheries.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.147
The English version gave the Queen

00:03:50.147 --> 00:03:54.630
"all the rights and powers of
sovereignty" over their land.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:55.770
In the Maori language,

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.710
there isn't a direct translation
for the word sovereignty

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.820
because there was no
singular ruler over the land.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.230
The British government
insisted the treaty placed

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.820
the Maori under British authority.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.320
In our culture, if you
want to include people

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.100
in the culture, you give them over land

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.760
so that they will become part of you.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.930
Well, the British
misinterpreted that as being

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.480
that we had sold our lands to
them and given everything up,

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.690
which simply just wasn't true.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:28.867
There was a great deal of
land initially, for settlement

00:04:28.867 --> 00:04:33.580
and the Crown acquired it
mostly through purchasing,

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.870
but the purchases were
forced purchases, basically

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.370
and the

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:44.262
promises made to Maori were

00:04:44.262 --> 00:04:45.970
essentially broken

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.430
and then when that land
started to run out,

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:53.130
then the Crown invaded
areas that were resisting.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:58.130
So essentially, we have
typical colonization procedures

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.378
to alienate Maori land and

00:05:01.378 --> 00:05:03.750
to marginalize Maori culture

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:08.070
and to try and assimilate
Maori into the Western ways

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:12.790
of thinking and seeing and doing.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:16.020
Wars, Western diseases, poverty;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:20.210
the effects of colonization
decimated the Maori population.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.780
Estimates suggest there
might've been 100,000 Maori

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:24.960
by the end of the 18th century.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.430
The 1896 census counted just 42,000.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.070
Within a generation the Maori
went from being landlords

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.025
for the White settlers
to being their laborers

00:05:35.025 --> 00:05:38.420
and all at the hands of
their own government.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.500
It wasn't just resources
that they took off us,

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.880
they tried to destroy
us as well as a people,

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.283
take away our language,
take away our culture,

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.140
write legislation making them illegal,

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.310
not allowed to be taught in schools.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:56.330
My father and his generation
were all physically punished

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.800
and physiologically punished
if they spoke Maori in schools.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.633
That's one of the reasons

00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:05.790
that we have such difficulty
in shifting the status quo,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:08.460
is that the British came out here

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.283
to seek their fortune in a
land that was not their own

00:06:12.283 --> 00:06:17.283
and in the process of doing
so became very privileged

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.050
very wealthy, very powerful

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.713
and not prepared to give that up.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:28.210
New Zealand used to pride
itself on being the country

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.210
with the best race relations in the world

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:35.496
and from about the 1960s, 1970s onwards,

00:06:35.496 --> 00:06:38.673
people increasingly challenged that.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.140
After the Second World War,

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.170
Maori began to migrate to the cities

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.820
in search of employment and modern life.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.240
The speed of this transformation
highlighted the gulf

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.190
between the two sets of people.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.900
Maori experienced discrimination

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.010
in housing and employment,
were expected to learn English

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.441
and abandon their customs.

00:06:57.441 --> 00:06:58.460
But this new generation

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.940
of urban Maori would have a huge impact.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:05.940
Maori in the cities had much more capacity

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.700
to challenge the Crown
and to get the Crown

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.220
to actually listen to their grievances,

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.747
particularly younger
Maori, who were educated

00:07:14.747 --> 00:07:19.270
and knew how to make their
way in the White world,

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:20.530
the Pakeha world.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:23.210
Younger Maori were terribly influenced

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.210
by the protest movements
right around the world

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:30.180
in the 1960s and including
Black power in America,

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.767
the American Indian Movement.

00:07:32.767 --> 00:07:36.490
But then the Maori land march in 1975,

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:38.478
that was tremendously important

00:07:38.478 --> 00:07:41.700
at bringing Maori issues
to Pakeha attention

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.700
and that was the combination of elders

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.091
led by a very revered kuia female elder,

00:07:48.091 --> 00:07:50.207
Dame Whina Cooper

00:07:50.207 --> 00:07:53.957
and in association with a lot of younger,

00:07:53.957 --> 00:07:55.055
more radical people.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:57.710
Suffering with arthritis,

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.620
80-year-old Whina Cooper led 5,000 people

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:04.137
on a 700-mile march to
protest stolen Maori land.

00:08:04.137 --> 00:08:05.630
Where's the justice?

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.270
This period of cultural
and political revival

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.850
is now known as the 'Maori Renaissance'.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:13.960
It helped unify Maori
opinion on land reclamation

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.940
and it opened the eyes
of White New Zealanders

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.104
to Maori causes and race more broadly.

00:08:19.104 --> 00:08:22.060
You started getting some
sort of understanding

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.710
amongst the general population

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.809
that things weren't
all that good for Maori

00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:32.711
and maybe that did explain
why Maori were so poor

00:08:32.711 --> 00:08:35.877
and so not able to keep up

00:08:35.877 --> 00:08:38.494
with the rest of the country.

00:08:38.494 --> 00:08:42.367
But there was no understanding
of how deliberate

00:08:42.367 --> 00:08:46.200
and how the bad faith that was involved

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.642
in what the British did,

00:08:48.642 --> 00:08:50.550
was simply not there.

00:08:51.691 --> 00:08:53.210
The same year as the Land March,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:56.050
the New Zealand government
launched the Waitangi Tribunal

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:00.900
to investigate contemporary
breaches of the 1840 treaty.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.150
Maori culture was being
revitalized and better protected.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:07.990
Huge numbers of New
Zealanders also protested

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:10.350
the visit of the South African rugby team,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.681
who maintained the segregationist
policy of apartheid.

00:09:14.681 --> 00:09:17.354
As both national and
international attention increased,

00:09:17.354 --> 00:09:19.190
the New Zealand government amended

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:20.830
the Treaty of Waitangi Act

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:23.060
to investigate historical grievances

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:26.080
dating back to the treaty of 1840.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.480
They began to realize that

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.960
if you have a large indigenous population

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:35.420
that's dissatisfied
with fundamental aspects

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:38.261
of the way you're running the country

00:09:38.261 --> 00:09:40.019
and the way you have run it,

00:09:40.019 --> 00:09:43.050
then the only way forward
in a meaningful way

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.898
is to address their grievances.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.400
By 1985 the tribunal was hearing claims

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.100
from individual tribes

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.760
and started making settlements varying

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:55.343
in size and substance

00:09:55.343 --> 00:09:56.176
and offering more

00:09:56.176 --> 00:09:58.430
than just monetary compensation.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:01.290
Some included formal
apologies from the Crown.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:04.450
The apology was tremendously
important for Maori

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:07.890
and the initial tribes we negotiated with,

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.580
they insisted on an apology

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:14.480
and the Crown was incredibly
reluctant to do so.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.600
They probably thought,
well, they certainly thought

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:20.470
that there might be legal
ramifications for that.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.800
Some claims had cultural significance

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.100
such as the restoration of Maori names

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.620
of geographical features.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.380
One iwi had so much
economic success resulting

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.050
from its settlement agreement

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.960
that it became a blueprint
for others to follow.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:38.276
The Ngai Tahu loss, economic loss

00:10:38.276 --> 00:10:42.884
was valued at 20 billion, 20 billion

00:10:42.884 --> 00:10:47.884
and we settled on the cash
offer of the 170 million.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.420
The Ngai Tahu land in the South Island,

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.186
which is equivalent in size to

00:10:52.186 --> 00:10:53.369
the State of New York,

00:10:53.369 --> 00:10:58.369
was taken by the British for just £14,750.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:00.870
Our people were split.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:05.170
There were those who
did not want to settle

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.459
because the quantum,

00:11:10.459 --> 00:11:12.284
the value that we were settling for

00:11:12.284 --> 00:11:16.695
was significantly less than what

00:11:16.695 --> 00:11:20.654
the assessment of what the
value should have been.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.620
Despite the discrepancy in value,

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:29.220
the Ngai Tahu have built
a successful portfolio

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:33.000
and intergenerational wealth
through savvy investment.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:36.170
As of May 2019, Ngai Tahu's fund boasts

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:39.420
an average annual return of more than 15%,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.930
according to the iwis investment arm.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.220
We're relatively now a wealthy tribe

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:49.860
with net settlement assets
of close to 1.6 billion.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.490
Today, Ngai Tahu has
interests in fishing, tourism,

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:56.948
property, as well as a diversified

00:11:56.948 --> 00:11:59.120
equities portfolio,

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:00.380
all of which are managed

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:04.338
through Ngai Tahu Holdings Limited,

00:12:04.338 --> 00:12:05.610
our commercial company.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:09.610
Structurally, there are two
parts to the organization.

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:12.730
So we have one arm that makes the money

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:15.620
and we have another arm
that spends the money

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:18.250
and we keep them very separate.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:22.300
The settlement at that time
was a relief for our people.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.830
It meant that our grievances
were acknowledged.

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:27.980
It meant the wrongdoings of
the Crown were acknowledged.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:31.960
It meant that we could
finally have redress

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:36.530
that could assist us to
develop a strong economic base

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:38.080
to support our people

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.690
and for our elders, the
apology part of settlement,

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.170
meant that they felt that our tupuna,

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:47.226
our ancestors before us,

00:12:47.226 --> 00:12:49.510
the wrongdoings had been acknowledged.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:52.720
Today, we are strong
enough and honest enough

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:57.720
to learn the lessons of the last 150 years

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:02.143
and to admit that the treaty
has been imperfectly observed.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:04.920
Despite wide acceptance of the need

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.970
for settlement agreements,
the process is found wanting

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:10.160
from both sides of the divide.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.640
Some criticize the increasing
number of claims and expense.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.010
Many Maori argue
settlements don't even begin

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.040
to scratch the surface of their losses.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.670
The amount that they
were offering to settle

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:24.920
these claims was minuscule.

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:29.145
I'm talking 1% of what was lost

00:13:29.145 --> 00:13:34.060
and no consideration at all
for the socioeconomic damage

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:35.340
that had been done.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:37.565
The grinding poverty

00:13:37.565 --> 00:13:42.224
and the grinding
marginalization, deprivation,

00:13:42.224 --> 00:13:45.220
just became too much for our people

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:49.717
and so they started
accepting just a little bit

00:13:49.717 --> 00:13:54.033
to get themselves up off
the grinding poverty.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.950
And once they started doing that,

00:13:56.950 --> 00:14:00.570
the government machine was relentless

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:03.650
and it just drove settlements

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:05.920
and I was one of the negotiators.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:10.190
My iwi decided we would try to see

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:11.540
if we could get our lands back

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.120
and under the settlement process,

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:19.090
it was the most horrific, demoralizing

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:21.420
process that I've ever been through.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.070
They would come into your iwi,

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:26.092
divide your iwi so that you're
all fighting each other.

00:14:26.092 --> 00:14:29.550
And that was the biggest
damage that was done,

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:32.810
was the internal conflict that it caused

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.443
and all of that for the return of

00:14:35.443 --> 00:14:39.584
1% of your lands.

00:14:39.584 --> 00:14:42.060
Settling is also
contentious for some Maori

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:44.438
who aspires to rangatiratanga,

00:14:44.438 --> 00:14:46.760
which translates as the
right of Maori people

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.953
to rule themselves.

00:14:48.953 --> 00:14:52.200
Another issue was how the
Crown could give away land.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.960
Owners of private land who
gained it through legal means

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.850
cannot have it taken from them.

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:58.770
The Crown can only give its own land

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:01.912
or money for the tribes to purchase land.

00:15:01.912 --> 00:15:03.820
It had to offer packages
that could never fully

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:06.280
compensate the tribes,
but were large enough

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.106
to help them rebuild.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.570
They also had to be kept in
line with the other tribes,

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:14.069
of which they are around 100.

00:15:14.069 --> 00:15:17.170
Some iwi aren't close to settling either.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:20.010
The claims process is far from perfect

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:22.704
and will always attract conflicting views.

00:15:22.704 --> 00:15:25.100
But what the majority agree

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:28.080
is that it offers a roadmap
on how to acknowledge events

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.110
of the past and build for the future.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:34.508
What extends beyond the negotiating table?

00:15:34.508 --> 00:15:36.870
That is not the end of our relationship

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:40.460
nor is it the end of the
Crown's responsibility.

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:43.283
It's the fact that not
everyone has a decent home,

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:46.993
a decent place to live

00:15:46.993 --> 00:15:50.810
and it's the incarceration

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:55.210
of the Maori people
disproportionately to everyone else.

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:58.260
That is the distance between us

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.730
and so long as that exists,

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:03.570
we have failed in our partnership,

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:08.150
but I inherently believe
in our power to change.

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:12.806
I look back to the time
before treaty settlements

00:16:12.806 --> 00:16:16.800
when Maori were in fear

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:20.340
of getting anything much
at all out of the Crown,

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:23.870
any kind of respect for rangatiratanga

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:28.000
and where Pakeha thought

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.900
that we had perfect race relations.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:32.860
There wasn't much of a problem.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:37.770
I think most New Zealanders
of European descent

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:40.560
wouldn't think that now at all.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.500
They know that there was
a typical colonial past

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:48.010
for New Zealand and that Maori were

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:53.010
disenfranchised and
disadvantaged and marginalized.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.670
While systemic issues
remain in New Zealand,

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:58.320
settlements have revitalized communities,

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.520
increased intergenerational wealth

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:03.426
and reconciled differences.

00:17:03.426 --> 00:17:04.890
The Maori economy is now worth

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.660
almost 70 billion New Zealand dollars.

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:11.170
That's up from an estimated
50 billion in 2013.

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:14.750
I don't wanna kind of leave
it with too rosy a view,

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:18.760
but I do think that we
have made enormous progress

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:21.620
and we're on quite a good platform

00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:24.863
for better progress still.

